            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Genesis of the Tripartite Struggle</h3>\n<p>Between the <strong>mid-8th and late 10th centuries AD</strong>, northern Indian history was dominated by an unrelenting triangular conflict among three formidable regional imperial powers:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Gurjara-Pratiharas (Western India &amp; Upper Doab):</strong> Originated in Rajasthan and Gujarat (Avanti/Mandor), serving as a bulwark against Arab expansion.</li>\n  <li><strong>Palas (Eastern India):</strong> Centered in Bengal and Bihar, holding deep agrarian wealth and vast war-elephant resources.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rashtrakutas (Deccan):</strong> Centered at Manyakheta (Malkhed, Karnataka) in the Deccan, commanding formidable armies and cavalry.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Prize: Why Kannauj?</h3>\n<p>Control of <strong>Kannauj</strong> conferred paramountcy over the central Gangetic plains (the <em>Antarvedi</em> doab). It was both an ideological claim to the ancient universal status of <strong>Chakravartin</strong> and an economic lifeline controlling the silk and riverine trade routes linking the Himalayas to the Bay of Bengal.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Major Phases of the Conflict</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>First Phase (c. 780–800 AD):</strong> Vatsaraja (Pratihara) defeated Dharmapala (Pala) in the Doab. However, <strong>Dhruva Dharavarsha (Rashtrakuta)</strong> marched north, crushed Vatsaraja, defeated Dharmapala, and briefly occupied Kannauj before returning south. Dharmapala installed his nominee <em>Chakrayudha</em> on the Kannauj throne.</li>\n  <li><strong>Second Phase (c. 800–836 AD):</strong> Nagabhata II (Pratihara) overthrew Chakrayudha and seized Kannauj. But <strong>Govinda III (Rashtrakuta)</strong> launched another lightning northern blitzkrieg, defeating Nagabhata II and receiving submission from Dharmapala. Once again, distance and Deccan politics forced the Rashtrakutas to withdraw.</li>\n  <li><strong>Third Phase &amp; Pratihara Ascendancy (c. 836–910 AD):</strong> <strong>Mihir Bhoja (836–885 AD)</strong>, the greatest Pratihara monarch, established Kannauj as his permanent capital, consolidating an empire stretching from Punjab to Bengal border. His son Mahendrapala I further expanded eastward into Bihar.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Climax &amp; Mutual Exhaustion:</strong> In 916 AD, <strong>Indra III (Rashtrakuta)</strong> sacked Kannauj. Though the Pratiharas recovered the city under Mahipala, their authority was mortally fractured, spawning splintered Rajput clans (Chauhans, Paramaras, Chandellas).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>4. Historical Significance</h3>\n<p>The two centuries of ceaseless warfare drained the resources, treasuries, and armies of all three indigenous dynasties. When the Ghaznavid Turks arrived in the early 11th century, Northern India was hopelessly fragmented without a unified imperial defense.</p>\n",
