            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Rise of Gopala &amp; End of 'Matsyanyaya'</h3>\n<p>Following the death of King Shashanka in the early 7th century, Bengal was ravaged for a century by internal civil strife and external invasions. The <strong>Khalimpur copper plate inscription</strong> of Dharmapala records that the people/chieftains elected <strong>Gopala (c. 750 AD)</strong> as king to terminate <strong>Matsyanyaya</strong> ('the law of the fishes', where big fish devour small fish in the absence of law).</p>\n\n<h3>2. Dharmapala (c. 770–810 AD): Imperial Apex</h3>\n<p>Son of Gopala, Dharmapala transformed the kingdom into a pan-North Indian empire:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Kannauj Durbar:</strong> Marched into the Doab, ousted Indrayudha, and installed Chakrayudha at Kannauj in a glittering durbar celebrated in contemporary literature. The 11th-century Gujarati poet Soddhala hailed him as <strong>Uttarapathasvamin</strong> ('Lord of Northern India').</li>\n  <li><strong>Foundation of Vikramshila:</strong> Founded the renowned <strong>Vikramshila Mahavihara</strong> (near modern Antichak, Bhagalpur, Bihar) on the banks of the Ganga. It grew into one of the greatest international centers of Vajrayana Buddhist learning, featuring 108 professors and attracting scholars from Tibet and Central Asia.</li>\n  <li><strong>Somapura Mahavihara:</strong> Built the colossal monastic complex at Paharpur (Bangladesh), the largest Buddhist vihara south of the Himalayas.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Devapala (c. 810–850 AD): Military Zenith</h3>\n<p>Under Devapala, Pala military expansion reached its outer territorial frontier, conquering Kamarupa (Assam) and subjugating Utkala (Odisha). The <strong>Badal pillar inscription</strong> praises his conquests from the Himalayas to Kanyakumari.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>International Buddhist Diplomacy:</strong> King Balaputradeva of the maritime <strong>Sailendra Empire of Suvarnadvipa (Sumatra/Java)</strong> sent an embassy to Devapala requesting the grant of five villages for the endowment of a Buddhist monastery at <strong>Nalanda</strong>, which Devapala granted through the <em>Nalanda Copper Plate</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Pala Intellectual &amp; Cultural Renaissance</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Atisa Dipankara (980–1054 AD):</strong> One of Vikramshila's most distinguished scholars; traveled to Tibet at the invitation of the Tibetan king to reform and re-establish Buddhism (Lamaism).</li>\n  <li><strong>Pala Bronze &amp; Stone Sculpture:</strong> Developed a distinctive school of bronze casting and black basalt stone carving. Master craftsmen <strong>Dhiman and his son Vitpala</strong> produced elegant Buddhist and Brahmanical images renowned for delicate modeling and polished finish.</li>\n</ul>\n",
