            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>Rise of the Imperial Cholas</h4>\n        <p>The Chola dynasty emerged from the shadows of early Sangam antiquity to establish one of the most powerful maritime empires in Indian history during the 9th to 13th centuries. The revival began with <strong>Vijayalaya</strong> (c. 850 AD), a feudatory of the Pallavas, who captured Thanjavur from the Muttaraiyars and consecrated a temple to goddess Nishumbhasudini.</p>\n\n        <h4>Rajaraja I (985–1014 AD): The Architect of Imperial Grandeur</h4>\n        <p>Known as <em>Arumolivarman</em> before his coronation, <strong>Rajaraja I</strong> transformed the Chola kingdom into a centralized military and naval superpower:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Military Campaigns:</strong> Destroyed the Chera fleet at Kandalur Salai, annexed northern Sri Lanka (naming it <em>Mummudi-Chola-Mandalam</em> with its capital at Polonnaruwa), subdued the Western Chalukyas of Kalyani under Satyashraya, and conquered the Maldive islands (<em>Munnir Palantivu Pannirayiram</em>).</li>\n            <li><strong>Cadastral Revenue Survey (1001 AD):</strong> Instituted an exhaustive, state-wide land survey and measurement operation. Lands were classified based on soil fertility, irrigation facilities, and yield, fixing the state revenue demand accurately.</li>\n            <li><strong>Meykkirtti (Preambles):</strong> Pioneered the practice of inscribing historical introductions (<em>meykkirtti</em>) at the beginning of all stone inscriptions, detailing his military conquests and institutional acts in chronological verse.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>The Brihadisvara Temple at Thanjavur (Rajarajeswaram)</h4>\n        <p>Completed in <strong>1010 AD</strong>, this colossal Dravidian temple dedicated to Lord Shiva stands as a triumph of Chola imperial architecture:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Features a towering 13-storey pyramidal <em>vimana</em> rising to 66 meters (216 feet), capped by a monolithic octagonal granite dome (<em>sikhara</em>) weighing approximately 80 tons.</li>\n            <li>Unlike earlier Pallava temples where the entrance gateway was modest, Rajaraja introduced massive multi-tiered entrance towers called <em>gopurams</em> (e.g., Keralantakan and Rajarajan Tiruvassal).</li>\n            <li>The temple walls feature exquisite fresco murals depicting Lord Shiva as Tripurantaka and Rajaraja I with his spiritual guru Karuvur Devar, showcasing advanced Chola visual arts.</li>\n        </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Vijayalaya re-established Chola power around 850 AD by seizing Thanjavur from the Muttaraiyars.",
                "Rajaraja I initiated the practice of prefixing stone inscriptions with official historical preambles (meykkirttis).",
                "Brihadisvara Temple (completed 1010 AD) at Thanjavur features a 66m granite vimana with an 80-ton monolithic sikhara.",
                "Rajaraja conducted an exhaustive cadastral land survey in 1001 AD to systematize imperial land revenue assessment."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Imperial Chola administrative division: Mandalams (Provinces) → Valanadus (Divisions) → Nadus (Districts) → Urs / Gramams (Villages)."
            ],
