            "title": "Sacking of Somnath Temple (1026 AD) & Northern Geopolitics",
            "summary": "Mahmud's 16th expedition through the Thar desert, the defense and fall of Somnath in Saurashtra, temple riches, Bhima I of the Solankis, and the long-term geopolitical impacts on northern India.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>The 16th Expedition: Target Somnath (1025–1026 AD)</h4>\n        <p>The most famous, daring, and lucrative raid undertaken by Mahmud of Ghazni was his assault on the celebrated temple of <strong>Somnath</strong>, situated on the coast of Saurashtra (Veraval, Gujarat):</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Religious & Economic Standing:</strong> Somnath was one of the twelve sacred <em>Jyotirlingas</em> of Lord Shiva. The shrine was sustained by the revenues of 10,000 villages, staffed by over 1,000 priests, and patronized by pilgrims from across the subcontinent. Its sanctum housed a famous Shivalinga that appeared suspended in mid-air (sustained by magnetic stones embedded in the stone canopy).</li>\n            <li><strong>The March across the Desert:</strong> To avoid heavily fortified Rajput positions in Central India, Mahmud marched in late 1025 AD with 30,000 regular horsemen and thousands of volunteers across the waterless Thar Desert from Multan, through Jaisalmer and Anhilwara (Patan).</li>\n            <li>The Solanki (Chalukya) King of Gujarat, <strong>Bhima I (Mularaja II's ancestor)</strong>, evacuated his capital Anhilwara and took refuge in the island fort of Kanthkot, leaving Somnath defended by local garrisons and devout Shaivite defenders.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>The Siege and Plunder of Somnath (January 1026 AD)</h4>\n        <p>The defenders fought with desperate heroism on the ramparts for three days. After breach of the city walls, fierce hand-to-hand fighting led to the death of over 50,000 defenders:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Mahmud entered the sanctum sanctorum and shattered the revered Shiva lingam with his own mace, earning the epithet <strong>But-Shikan</strong> (Idol Breaker).</li>\n            <li>Pieces of the shattered idol were carted back to Ghazni and embedded in the steps of the Jami Masjid so that worshippers would tread upon them.</li>\n            <li>The temple yielded treasure estimated at over <strong>two million dinars</strong>, along with massive gold chandeliers, gemstone-studded silver chains, and jeweled canopies.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>The Perilous Retreat and Last Expedition (1027 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Fearing interception by a confederacy of Rajput rulers led by Bhima I and the Paramaras of Malwa, Mahmud retreated along the inhospitable Rann of Kutch and Sindh desert:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>During the retreat, treacherous local guides led his army into waterless salt pans, causing immense loss of pack animals and soldiers.</li>\n            <li>The <strong>Jats of the Salt Range / Sindh</strong> repeatedly harassed and plundered Mahmud's baggage train during this retreat.</li>\n            <li><strong>17th Expedition (1027 AD):</strong> Mahmud's final expedition was a punitive naval and land campaign against the Jats of the Indus River, deploying 1,400 armored river boats equipped with iron spikes.</li>\n            <li>Mahmud passed away at Ghazni in 1030 AD at the age of 59, leaving behind an empire stretching from the Oxus to the Indus, but no permanent institutional governance in India outside of Punjab.</li>\n        </ul>\n        ",
